1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:04,960
Start by locating the workspace where the
coding agent is allowed to operate.

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:10,348
In this course, Claude Code and OpenCode
run inside the corporate Google Cloud

3
00:00:10,348 --> 00:00:11,016
workspace.

4
00:00:11,196 --> 00:00:15,889
The terminal and repository shown here are
synthetic training examples.

5
00:00:16,069 --> 00:00:19,460
Keep your session inside the approved
remote workspace.

6
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:23,831
We will verify the environment before we
give either agent a task.

7
00:00:25,298 --> 00:00:29,624
In the approved terminal, check the
current directory, branch, and short

8
00:00:29,624 --> 00:00:30,705
commit identifier.

9
00:00:30,885 --> 00:00:35,397
These small checks tell you which checkout
the agent can see and where to find your

10
00:00:35,397 --> 00:00:36,539
starting point later.

11
00:00:36,719 --> 00:00:39,174
The example is clean on a lab branch.

12
00:00:39,354 --> 00:00:44,101
If your actual checkout contains someone
else's work, leave it in place and switch

13
00:00:44,101 --> 00:00:46,011
to an approved training checkout.

14
00:00:47,465 --> 00:00:52,724
Now check the versions of Claude Code and
OpenCode that the organization has

15
00:00:52,724 --> 00:00:53,416
installed.

16
00:00:53,596 --> 00:00:58,138
Features, selectors, and available
controls can change across versions.

17
00:00:58,318 --> 00:01:02,604
For that reason, the lab records what the
terminal actually reports.

18
00:01:02,784 --> 00:01:07,564
A missing command is an access or setup
question for the workspace owner.

19
00:01:07,744 --> 00:01:11,731
Use the client your organization provides
for this exercise.

20
00:01:13,173 --> 00:01:16,522
Inspect the model selection in the
authorized client.

21
00:01:16,702 --> 00:01:21,893
Record the exact model identifier if it is
available, along with the configured

22
00:01:21,893 --> 00:01:22,484
provider.

23
00:01:22,664 --> 00:01:27,573
The program that runs on the workstation,
the model that interprets the task, and

24
00:01:27,573 --> 00:01:31,997
the service that processes the request are
separate parts of the session.

25
00:01:32,177 --> 00:01:36,978
The workspace administrator can confirm
any setting that your account cannot see.

26
00:01:38,423 --> 00:01:43,715
This example gives the agent access to
read the training repository and asks for

27
00:01:43,715 --> 00:01:46,956
confirmation before editing or running its
tests.

28
00:01:47,136 --> 00:01:50,125
Other workspaces may apply a different
policy.

29
00:01:50,305 --> 00:01:54,475
Check the effective rules in the client
and the surrounding environment.

30
00:01:54,655 --> 00:01:59,730
Keep project guidance, tool permission,
and the runtime boundary separate when you

31
00:01:59,730 --> 00:02:01,897
reason about what the agent can do.

32
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:05,157
Start with a small inspection.

33
00:02:05,337 --> 00:02:10,007
Ask the agent to find the quantity
validator and the associated unit tests,

34
00:02:10,007 --> 00:02:11,938
then give you their file paths.

35
00:02:12,118 --> 00:02:17,387
This is a useful first task because you
can open each file to verify the result.

36
00:02:17,567 --> 00:02:22,607
If the client proposes an edit or command
outside the stated scope, pause and

37
00:02:22,607 --> 00:02:25,226
inspect the request before approving it.

38
00:02:26,673 --> 00:02:31,740
Open the cited file yourself and confirm
that it contains the relevant function.

39
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:33,865
Then run the status check again.

40
00:02:34,045 --> 00:02:38,323
In this inspection, the working tree
should look exactly as it did at the

41
00:02:38,323 --> 00:02:38,675
start.

42
00:02:38,855 --> 00:02:43,378
This final check gives you something
concrete to compare with the agent's

43
00:02:43,378 --> 00:02:47,591
summary, and it reinforces a habit we will
use before every handoff.

44
00:02:49,048 --> 00:02:53,932
Before ending, write down the workspace,
branch, starting commit, client version,

45
00:02:53,932 --> 00:02:55,620
model, and permission scope.

46
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:57,520
Keep the record concise.

47
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:02,805
You now have an auditable description of
the environment and a verified read-only

48
00:03:02,805 --> 00:03:03,246
result.

49
00:03:03,426 --> 00:03:08,172
In the next lab, that description will
help you compare how the two clients

50
00:03:08,172 --> 00:03:11,842
discover instructions and skills from the
same repository.
